    id: "s-gen-part2-a-boring-place-aug2026",
    title: "Describe a boring place",
    part: "part2",
    band: "8.0",
    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a boring place",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The place I want to describe is a public administration office near my apartment, where I once had to renew some paperwork and ended up spending nearly an entire afternoon waiting.\n\nFrom the outside it doesn't look unpleasant exactly — clean tiled floor, plastic chairs lined up in neat rows, a ticket machine dispensing numbers — but everything about the atmosphere felt deliberately drained of energy. The walls were a flat, institutional beige, the lighting buzzed faintly overhead, and the only sound for long stretches was the occasional beep of the number display changing, followed by a collective, slightly resigned shuffle as people checked their tickets again.\n\nI remember watching an old fan rotating slowly in the corner, apparently the only moving thing in the entire room, and genuinely losing track of time because there was nothing to focus on except my own boredom. People around me scrolled their phones with the specific, joyless expression of someone killing time rather than actually enjoying anything, occasionally sighing when a name got called out of order.\n\nWhat made it particularly boring wasn't just the waiting itself, but the complete absence of anything to look at or think about — no interesting noise, no interaction, no visual variety, just repetitive procedure. Even the staff behind the counters seemed to move with a kind of practiced, low-energy efficiency, processing forms without a flicker of enthusiasm.\n\nI finally got called after nearly two hours, and the actual task took about ninety seconds. That contrast — hours of flat, grey waiting followed by a task over almost instantly — is honestly what still makes me associate that place with boredom whenever I think back on it.",
