    band: "8.0",
    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a challenging technological problem you faced",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The technological problem I want to talk about happened a few months ago, while I was preparing a short video introduction for a job application that specifically required one instead of a normal cover letter. I'd spent an entire evening recording and re-recording it on my laptop, trying to sound natural rather than rehearsed, since first impressions clearly mattered for this particular role.\n\nRight after finally getting a version I was happy with, my laptop suddenly froze while I was exporting the file, and when I restarted it, the whole folder containing every single take had somehow become corrupted. I couldn't open a single clip, and none of them showed up properly in any video software I tried. To be honest, I panicked a bit, because the application deadline was the next morning, and I definitely didn't have the energy left to record everything again from scratch that same night.\n\nI spent almost two hours googling the error, trying different recovery programs, and messaging a friend who's more tech-savvy than me for advice. Eventually, one of the free recovery tools managed to restore about half the files, just enough that I could patch together something usable instead of starting completely over.\n\nWhat I took away from that whole mess is that convenience always comes with risk, especially when everything important lives on a single device. I've now switched to backing up recordings onto cloud storage immediately after filming instead of waiting, which honestly should have been obvious from the start. It was stressful in the moment, but it pushed me to build a habit I clearly needed anyway.",
