    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "If I had to choose a country to live and work in for a short period, I'd probably say Thailand, specifically Chiang Mai, even though I should admit upfront that I'm not naturally a big traveller. I get motion sickness fairly easily, and long, unstructured trips have never really appealed to me the way they seem to for a lot of people.\n\nWhat makes Chiang Mai appealing despite that is the idea of a short, low-pressure stay rather than an ambitious backpacking adventure. Since I'm currently between jobs after leaving my old office role, the timing actually fits — maybe two or three months, no fixed career plan involved, just picking up something simple like helping out at a small independent café or bookstore while I figure out my next real step back home. Chiang Mai has its own international airport, so getting there would mean a direct flight, and once I arrived, I could rely on local Grab bikes to get around the old city instead of unfamiliar traffic in a car.\n\nI'm also drawn to the slower pace there — the old town's narrow lanes and its quiet cafés that seem built for people who just want to sit and read for hours, close to my idea of a perfect afternoon.\n\nI wouldn't want it to be permanent, though. I still prefer my own apartment in Ho Chi Minh City, and I know novelty wears off quickly for someone like me. But a few contained months somewhere calm, reachable by a short flight and easy to get around by Grab bike, feels like the right dose of \"abroad\" I could actually enjoy.",
