    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The difficult decision I want to describe is turning down a full-time office job offer about three months ago, even though I'd been unemployed for a while and money was getting genuinely tight. It sounds like a strange thing to refuse when you're job-hunting, but at the time it felt like the right call, despite how risky it seemed.\n\nThe role itself was fine on paper — decent salary, normal hours, a respectable company — but during the final interview, I realised it was essentially a slightly better version of the exact job I'd left months earlier: same repetitive admin tasks, same rigid structure, same lack of room to actually think for myself. My parents thought I was being fussy, since a steady salary was sitting right there, and honestly, part of me agreed with them out of pure anxiety about my shrinking savings.\n\nI turned it down anyway, mostly because I could already picture myself resigning again in another year, exhausted in exactly the same way. Instead, I kept applying selectively and started helping out part-time at a friend's small café on weekends, just to earn something while I figured out my next move properly.\n\nThe result turned out better than expected. Within two months, I landed a role that actually matched what I wanted, with far more flexibility, and the café weekends gave me hands-on experience that's now feeding directly into my own long-term plan to open a small place like that myself one day. Looking back, refusing that \"safe\" offer was uncomfortable, but it stopped me from repeating a mistake I already knew the ending to.",
