    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a famous person you would like to meet",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "If I had to pick one famous person I'd genuinely like to meet, it would be Nguyễn Nhật Ánh, the Vietnamese author whose books I grew up reading and still return to occasionally, even now as an adult trying to figure out what to do with my life.\n\nWhat first drew me to his writing, long before I thought of him as \"famous,\" was simply how ordinary and specific his stories felt — small-town childhoods, awkward first crushes, the kind of quiet nostalgia that never seemed exaggerated for drama's sake. As someone who's always loved reading more than almost anything else, discovering an author who wrote about Vietnamese life so honestly, without imitating Western storytelling styles, genuinely shaped how I think about books.\n\nIf I actually got the chance to meet him, I wouldn't really want the typical fan moment of just asking for a signed copy. I'd want to ask how he decided, at some point, to commit fully to writing rather than treating it as a side hobby alongside something more \"stable\" — a question that feels oddly relevant to me right now, since I'm seriously considering whether to chase my own small, less conventional dream of running a bookstore-café instead of returning to office work.\n\nI doubt this will happen realistically, since he's fairly private and rarely does public appearances these days. But if some coincidence ever put us in the same room, maybe at a book signing, I think I'd walk away with something more useful than an autograph — a slightly clearer sense of how people actually commit to what they love, rather than just talking about it.",
