    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The dish I want to describe is thịt kho trứng, sometimes called thịt kho tàu — braised pork belly and eggs in a slightly sweet caramel-fish-sauce broth — which almost every southern Vietnamese family, including mine, cooks specifically around Tết.\n\nMy mum usually starts the pot two or three days before New Year's Eve, using a big claypot that only comes out of storage once a year. The pork gets simmered slowly with coconut water, fish sauce, and boiled eggs until everything turns a deep amber colour, and the whole apartment smells like caramel and pork fat for days afterwards. Honestly, as a kid I found the smell a bit much, but now it's basically the smell of Tết itself for me — I can't separate the two anymore.\n\nWhat makes it a special-occasion dish, rather than everyday food, is partly the effort involved and partly the symbolism. The rounded eggs are meant to represent fullness and unity for the coming year, and having a pot ready means the family won't need to cook much during the first few days of Tết, when many people avoid using knives or lighting the stove too often, following old superstitions about starting the year gently.\n\nWe eat it with pickled vegetables and rice, usually reheating the same pot for several days, and somehow it tastes better each time the flavours deepen. Even now that I live alone, I still go back to my parents' place every Tết partly for this dish — some food genuinely belongs to a specific time of year, and thịt kho trứng, for me, is exactly that.",
