    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The friend I want to describe is Tuan, who lived three houses down from mine in the alley where I grew up, here in Ho Chi Minh City. We were basically inseparable between the ages of maybe eight and thirteen, which feels like a lifetime ago now.\n\nBack then, our whole world was that narrow alley. Almost every afternoon after school, we'd play badminton right there in front of his house, using the space between two motorbikes as the \"court,\" and honestly half our shuttlecocks ended up on somebody's roof. When it rained, which was often, we'd just move inside and play cards or some silly board game instead, arguing over rules we clearly kept changing to whoever was losing.\n\nWhat made him a proper childhood friend, though, wasn't just the games — it was that he covered for me once when I broke a neighbour's flowerpot with a badly aimed shuttlecock and I panicked and blamed a stray cat, of all things. He backed up my ridiculous story without hesitation, even though he later admitted he thought it was a terrible lie.\n\nWe drifted apart naturally around secondary school, different classes, different friend groups, nothing dramatic. For years I barely thought about him until, randomly, his name popped up as a suggested contact on social media last year. We messaged a bit, laughed about the flowerpot story again, and agreed to grab coffee sometime, though we still haven't actually managed it.\n\nI don't know if we'll ever be close again the way we were, but that alley, that badminton \"court,\" and one loyal, terrible lie still feel like one of the warmest parts of growing up.",
