    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The law I'd like to describe is Vietnam's regulation on sorting household waste at source, which comes from the 2020 Law on Environmental Protection and has been rolled out more seriously in Ho Chi Minh City over the past couple of years.\n\nBasically, households are now supposed to separate rubbish into different categories — recyclable items like bottles and cardboard, organic waste like food scraps, and everything else — before collection workers pick it up. In theory, waste that's sorted properly can be recycled or composted instead of all going straight to a landfill, which is where most of the city's trash used to end up, creating serious overflow and pollution problems.\n\nIn practice, adjusting has been messy. My building only got proper labelled bins fairly recently, and even now, half the neighbours still throw everything into one bag out of habit or because it's simply faster. The collection workers sometimes complain that mixed-up bags make the sorting effort pointless anyway. I try to sort mine properly, mostly because I feel a bit guilty otherwise, though I won't pretend I'm perfectly consistent, especially on lazy days when I just want the rubbish out of my apartment.\n\nStill, I think the law matters even with messy execution. It signals that waste isn't something you can just forget about once the bin bag disappears, and it's slowly training people, especially younger residents who already worry about flooding and plastic pollution, to think one step further about where their rubbish actually goes. Given enough time and stricter enforcement, I genuinely believe habits here could shift for the better.",
