    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The live sports event I want to describe is a Vietnam national football match I watched at a small café near my apartment with a group of neighbours, during one of the regional tournaments last year.\n\nI'm not someone who follows sport closely most of the year, but football during a big tournament is different here — it's less about the sport itself and more about the shared atmosphere. A neighbour messaged the building group chat that morning inviting anyone free to watch together instead of alone, and since the café owner had set up an extra screen outside facing the street, a fairly large, mixed crowd ended up gathering by kickoff.\n\nThe match itself was tense from the start, low-scoring, with Vietnam defending for long stretches in the second half. What made it memorable wasn't really the football technique but the crowd's reactions — collective groaning at missed chances, someone's grandmother shouting instructions at the screen as if the players could hear her, motorbikes honking down the street the moment Vietnam finally scored near the end. Strangers were hugging each other, plastic stools got knocked over, and the café owner gave out free drinks once the final whistle confirmed the win.\n\nI don't usually enjoy loud, crowded places, but that night felt different, almost communal rather than overwhelming. Watching it at home alone would have technically shown me the same goals, but I don't think it would have left the same impression. It reminded me that sometimes the appeal of live sport isn't the game itself, but genuinely sharing that tension with the people around you.",
