    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The movie I'd like to talk about is \"Mai,\" a Vietnamese drama that came out a couple of years ago and that I finally got round to watching properly a few weeks back, one lazy Sunday when I genuinely had nothing else planned.\n\nI watched it at home, actually, curled up on the sofa with instant noodles instead of going to the cinema — going out just isn't really my thing, especially when streaming makes it so easy to watch something good from bed. The story follows a massage-parlour worker named Mai who's trying to rebuild her life and protect her daughter while carrying a fairly heavy past, and it slowly develops into an unexpected romance with her neighbour.\n\nWhat struck me most was how the film avoided turning Mai into either a tragic victim or an unrealistically strong heroine — she felt like a real person making compromised, human decisions under pressure, which is something Vietnamese cinema doesn't always pull off convincingly. The ending genuinely surprised me; it wasn't the neat, feel-good resolution I half expected, and I actually paused the film for a minute just to process it.\n\nI enjoyed it so much partly because of the acting, which felt understated rather than melodramatic, and partly because the social backdrop — debt, family pressure, judgment from neighbours — felt painfully familiar rather than exaggerated for drama's sake. I ended up recommending it to two close friends the following week, which almost never happens with movies I watch purely for myself. It's rare that a film manages to be both emotionally heavy and genuinely entertaining at the same time, but this one did.",
