    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "If I could introduce a new law in Vietnam, it would be a proper noise-control regulation for residential alleys, specifically targeting portable karaoke speakers — the loud \"kẹo kéo\" boxes people wheel around or set up right outside their houses in the evening.\n\nWhere I live, at least once or twice a week, someone nearby sets up a speaker after dinner and sings, sometimes reasonably, sometimes painfully off-key, for hours, often well past eleven at night. There's technically a general noise-disturbance rule already, mentioning quiet hours, but enforcement is basically nonexistent — nobody actually comes to check decibel levels, and calling the police over a neighbour singing feels socially awkward, almost petty, even when you're exhausted and trying to prepare lessons for the next morning.\n\nMy proposed law would set clearer, specific cut-off times for amplified sound in residential lanes, say ten at night on weekdays, backed by an actual fine that local ward officers could issue on the spot rather than a vague warning nobody follows up on. I'd also want an easy reporting method, maybe through an app, so residents don't have to confront a singing neighbour face to face just to get one quiet evening.\n\nI know plenty of people would grumble, since evening singing sessions are genuinely a beloved pastime for a lot of older residents here, and I don't want to sound like I'm attacking that culture entirely. But there has to be a middle ground between community joy and someone else's ability to sleep, and right now, the law simply isn't doing that balancing job.",
