    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I’d like to talk about my aunt, Lan. She lives in the same district as me in Ho Chi Minh City, and I see her quite often at family dinners. To be honest, environmental protection still isn’t a hot everyday topic for a lot of people here — many just shrug if you bring it up. That said, I do notice some younger people in the city caring more these days, especially about plastic waste and flooding. My aunt isn’t part of that “young activist” group, though. She’s more old-school practical: she hates waste and mess, and that ends up helping the environment in a southern, down-to-earth way.\n\nFor example, she reuses the same plastic bags again and again until they fall apart, and she keeps bottles and cans for the scrap collector in the alley. She’s quick to complain when neighbours dump trash near the drain, especially in the rainy season when the lane floods. At home she finishes leftovers and switches off the air conditioner when nobody’s around — partly for the electricity bill, partly because wasting it feels silly. When my food-delivery orders leave a pile of containers, she’ll tease me with classic aunt energy: “Sao đặt nhiều vậy, ăn hết không?” — not a planet lecture, just blunt and familiar.\n\nI wouldn’t call her an environmentalist in the textbook sense. But next to how casually some people throw things away, her habits are quietly better for the place we live in. What I like is that it feels real for a woman her age in HCMC: thrift, cleanliness, neighbourly bluntness — while I also admit the younger generation is starting to talk about these issues a bit more openly. That’s why she came to mind.",
