    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The person I want to talk about is Huy, a friend I used to work with at my old office job before I left it. Out of everyone I know, he's easily the one who picks up languages the fastest, and it's honestly a little intimidating to be around sometimes.\n\nHuy speaks decent French, which he learned mostly through university, and he's been teaching himself Mandarin for about a year now using apps and dramas in his free time. What struck me was a company trip we both went on a couple of years ago, where several visiting partners from different countries showed up for a meeting. While the rest of us stuck to careful, textbook English, Huy casually switched into French with one of them, then later tried out some basic Mandarin phrases with another, stumbling a bit but clearly enjoying himself rather than being embarrassed.\n\nWhen I asked him how he manages it, he said he doesn't really \"study\" grammar rules the way most of us were taught at school — he just imitates sounds obsessively and isn't afraid to sound wrong in front of people, which honestly a lot of us, myself included, struggle with. He also keeps switching input: songs one week, a drama the next, voice notes from native speakers after that, so it never gets boring for him.\n\nI find him genuinely inspiring, partly because I'm someone who's fine with English but has zero talent for anything beyond it. Watching him treat mistakes so lightly has actually changed how relaxed I try to be whenever I practise a new skill myself these days.",
