    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about Bà Sáu, an older neighbour who lives two floors below my apartment. She's probably in her late sixties, and her whole balcony is basically taken over by pots of herbs and vegetables — mint, lemongrass, a few chilli plants, and something that looks like morning glory climbing along the railing.\n\nI first really noticed it about a year ago when I was ordering food delivery, as usual, and she happened to be watering her plants right as the driver pulled up. She laughed and said something like, \"Con lại đặt đồ ăn nữa hả,\" half teasing, and then handed me a small bag of fresh mint, insisting I use it instead of whatever came in the delivery bag. That's basically how our whole relationship works now — she grows, I get free herbs occasionally.\n\nFor her, it's not some trendy \"green lifestyle\" thing at all; it's purely practical. She grew up doing this in her hometown before moving to the city, and she keeps saying market vegetables have gotten expensive and she doesn't fully trust where they come from anyway. So growing her own means she always has something fresh for cooking, and it costs her almost nothing beyond soil and a bit of water every morning.\n\nWhat I admire is her patience. She checks those pots daily, no matter the weather, moving smaller ones out of harsh afternoon sun without complaining once. In a building full of people glued to their phones, myself included, she's the one person still getting her hands genuinely dirty, and somehow that small balcony garden makes the whole floor smell nicer.",
