    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The person I want to describe is Ngan, a close friend of mine since university, who is honestly the most organised human being I know. Where I mostly just wing things and hope for the best, Ngan seems to plan almost everything, down to details most people wouldn't even think about.\n\nWhat really showed me the extent of it was a short trip we took together to Da Lat last year. About three weeks before we even left, she'd already made a shared document with a full itinerary, backup options in case it rained, a rough budget split by category, and even a packing list for both of us, since she didn't trust me to remember sunscreen. I remember laughing at how excessive it seemed, especially the part where she'd colour-coded everything.\n\nOnce we were actually there, though, I understood the point. When one café we'd planned around unexpectedly closed early, she just calmly pulled up two backup spots from her document instead of us wasting an hour arguing about where to go, which is exactly what would have happened if I'd been in charge.\n\nIt's not only trips, either. She plans her weekly grocery list around what's on sale, blocks out specific study hours when she's preparing for exams instead of just \"studying whenever,\" and somehow still has time to relax without seeming stressed about any of it.\n\nI used to think that much planning would feel exhausting, but watching her, it actually looks like the opposite — like planning is what frees up her mental energy rather than using it up, which is something I'm slowly, and clumsily, trying to copy myself.",
