    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about my friend Ha, whom I used to work with at my old office before I left that job. She's probably the most reliably helpful person I've ever known, and it's not really a one-off thing — it's basically her default setting.\n\nThe clearest example is something she's done for almost two years now. On weekend mornings, when she's free, she tutors a small group of kids from lower-income families in her neighbourhood completely for free, using her own dining table as the classroom. I only found out because I saw a photo she'd posted almost by accident, of four kids crowded around worksheets, and when I asked about it she seemed a bit embarrassed, like it wasn't a big deal worth mentioning.\n\nAt our old workplace, it was the same pattern. Whenever a newer colleague struggled with a task or a difficult client email, Ha was usually the first one messaging back late at night with advice, even though technically none of that was her responsibility. I personally leaned on her more than once during a rough project, and I don't think I ever properly repaid the favour.\n\nWhat I admire most is that she never brings it up first or makes it sound like a sacrifice. When I once thanked her for staying late to help me fix a messy report, she just said something like, \"Ai cũng cần người giúp lúc rối, có gì đâu.\" That casual attitude, treating help as completely normal rather than something to be praised for, is honestly the part I try hardest to copy these days, especially now that I have more free time to actually offer it.",
