    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about Chu Sang, the technician who looks after the building where I rent my apartment. He's probably in his fifties, and most residents only really notice him when something breaks, which is exactly how I ended up appreciating how smart he actually is.\n\nLast year, during a stretch of really heavy rain, the power kept tripping on our floor almost every evening, right around the time I was in the middle of a video interview for a job I'd applied to. It was incredibly frustrating, since motion-sickness aside, I already avoid going out much, and now even staying home felt unreliable. Several of us complained to management, expecting the usual slow process of hiring an outside electrician who'd probably take a week to even show up.\n\nInstead, Chu Sang spent one afternoon quietly tracing the wiring himself, floor by floor, and figured out that a shared outdoor breaker box was getting slightly flooded whenever rain pooled near a blocked drain nearby, not because the wiring itself was faulty as everyone had assumed. Rather than waiting for a costly rewiring job that management kept postponing, he built a simple raised, sheltered cover for that specific box using scrap materials from a storage room, and separately convinced the building to finally unblock that one drain.\n\nThe whole fix probably cost almost nothing compared to what an official contractor would have charged, and the outages stopped completely afterwards. What impressed me wasn't advanced knowledge, honestly, since it wasn't complicated engineering at all. It was that he actually investigated properly instead of guessing, which meant he solved the real problem instead of the expensive one everybody assumed it was.",
