    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I'd like to talk about Chi, a close friend from university who now works at a large Vietnamese tech company here in Ho Chi Minh City — the kind that shows up in the news whenever it expands abroad or launches something new.\n\nWhen we graduated, Chi actually started at a small local start-up that barely had a proper office, just a rented room with a handful of desks. She used to complain about unpaid overtime and how nobody really knew what they were doing yet. I remember thinking she'd probably switch industries eventually. Instead, she stuck with it, kept applying and learning, and about three years ago she joined her current company, which had already grown into one of the bigger names in the sector by then.\n\nWhat struck me recently was meeting her for coffee after she got promoted to team lead. She described the company almost like a small ecosystem — proper training programmes, mentors assigned to juniors, actual budget for projects instead of everyone improvising like before. She also mentioned perks I honestly don't have right now while job-hunting, like health insurance covering her whole family and yearly trips abroad for top performers.\n\nListening to her, I felt a mix of admiration and mild envy, if I'm honest, especially given my own uncertain situation lately. But what stood out most wasn't the company's size — it was that she'd been patient enough to grow alongside it from those messy start-up days, rather than jumping around chasing a bigger name every year like a lot of people our age tend to do.",
