    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I'll talk about my uncle Bao, my dad's younger brother, who runs an online clothing shop that's grown a lot bigger than anyone in the family expected. He's based here in Ho Chi Minh City, and I actually order from his shop myself sometimes, which feels a bit strange but also convenient.\n\nHe started small, just reselling a few shirts through a personal Facebook page while still working a regular office job. Back then it was really just a side hustle, nothing serious. Everything changed around the pandemic, when people, including me, got used to ordering pretty much everything online instead of going to physical stores. Uncle Bao noticed that shift early and quit his job to focus on the shop full-time, which honestly worried my dad quite a bit at first, since it seemed like a risky decision.\n\nWhat actually made it successful, though, wasn't luck. He got serious about the boring parts most people skip — tracking which designs sold out fastest, replying to customer messages at almost any hour, and building a small delivery network so orders reached buyers within a day or two, even during peak seasons like Tet. Now he has a proper warehouse instead of a spare room, and a handful of staff packing orders.\n\nWhenever we meet at family dinners, he still complains about competitors copying his designs within days, so it's clearly not stress-free. But I respect that he turned something that started as a casual side project into an actual company, especially since he did it without any business background, just persistence and paying attention to what customers actually wanted.",
