    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about my friend Trang, who successfully opened her own small bakery-café last year after quitting a stable office job that everyone, including her own parents, thought she was foolish to leave.\n\nWe used to work at neighbouring companies in the same building and met years ago through mutual colleagues. She'd been unhappy in her marketing job for a long time, always talking about baking as \"just a hobby,\" until one day she finally admitted she wanted to try turning it into something real. The difficult part wasn't just quitting — it was everything afterwards: finding an affordable space, learning food safety rules, and testing recipes that would actually sell rather than just impress friends at parties, all while surviving on almost no income for the first few months.\n\nI remember visiting her half-finished shop once, paint cans everywhere, and she looked genuinely exhausted, half-joking that she might have made the biggest mistake of her life. There were weeks she considered going back to office work entirely, especially when a supplier let her down right before opening weekend. But she kept adjusting instead of giving up — changing her menu, her pricing, even her opening hours based on customer feedback.\n\nNow, over a year later, her little shop has a small but loyal group of regulars, and she genuinely looks happier than she ever did behind a desk. Watching her push through that uncertainty, especially since I'm currently figuring out my own next step, reminded me that difficult achievements rarely look dramatic from the outside — mostly just stubborn adjustment, repeated until something finally holds.",
