    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The plan I had to change recently was a small day out with a friend to scout café and bookstore spaces around the city, since I've been seriously considering opening a small shop of my own once I've saved enough.\n\nWe'd been planning it for almost two weeks — mapping out four or five neighbourhoods, booking nothing formal, but roughly timing which streets to walk through in the morning and where to stop for lunch. I genuinely looked forward to it, partly because getting out and actually seeing spaces felt more useful than endlessly scrolling rental listings online from my apartment.\n\nThen, on the Saturday morning we'd chosen, it rained heavily for hours, and by midday half the streets we'd planned to walk through were reportedly flooded, including one narrow lane my friend needed to cross on her motorbike. She sent me a photo of water almost reaching the pedals, clearly not something worth risking just to look at empty shopfronts.\n\nI made the call fairly quickly — cancel the walking plan entirely and switch to researching the same neighbourhoods online instead, using satellite maps and rental listing photos while messaging each other our thoughts. It wasn't nearly as useful as actually standing inside a space, and I was honestly disappointed, but running around flooded streets for a \"maybe someday\" business idea felt genuinely unwise.\n\nLooking back, I don't regret the change at all. If anything, it confirmed something I already suspected: fixed plans in this city always need a flexible backup, especially once rainy season starts, business scouting included.",
