    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to describe something that happened on my work laptop about a year ago, back when I was still at my old office job, right in the middle of finishing an important monthly report before a deadline. That laptop was basically my whole workstation — files, emails, spreadsheets, everything ran through it.\n\nThat afternoon, with barely two hours left before the deadline, my screen suddenly froze completely. The cursor stuck in one spot, nothing responded no matter what I clicked, and when I finally held the power button to force it off, the whole machine shut down with no warning message at all. When I turned it back on, it took an unusually long time to boot, and the spreadsheet I'd been working on for hours refused to open properly, showing a corrupted-file error instead.\n\nHonestly, I panicked a little, since I hadn't saved a backup copy anywhere else, trusting the auto-save feature completely. I quickly called our IT support, who talked me through a recovery attempt over the phone while my manager kept messaging asking for an update. Eventually, one of the recovery tools managed to restore most of the file, though I had to rebuild the last hour of work from memory and a few printed drafts.\n\nSince then, I've kept a habit of saving important files to a separate drive constantly, not just trusting one device. It taught me something obvious but easy to ignore: convenience always comes with risk, and a backup plan isn't optional when your job depends on one machine.",
