    modelMonologue: "The app I've been using the most lately, apart from basic messaging ones, is a local job-hunting app that I check almost every single day. I only really started relying on it heavily after I left my previous office job a few months ago, and it's now practically part of my morning routine.\n\nI first downloaded it out of mild desperation, honestly, after scrolling through the same three or four company websites for weeks and getting nowhere. A friend who'd used it during her own job search recommended it, and once I set up filters for industry, salary range, and location, it started sending me far more relevant listings than I'd been finding manually.\n\nNow I use it for almost everything related to job-hunting: saving postings I like, tracking which companies I've already applied to, and reading candid reviews from former employees before deciding whether to bother applying at all. What I appreciate most is the built-in reminder feature — it nudges me to follow up if a company hasn't replied within two weeks, which I'd definitely forget to do on my own.\n\nThe other feature I rely on constantly is the salary comparison tool, which shows roughly what similar roles pay across the city, so I don't undersell myself out of nerves during negotiations. There's also a small community forum inside the app where people compare interview experiences at the same companies, which has genuinely calmed me down before a nerve-wracking call. Honestly, before this app, job-hunting felt like shouting into a void; now it at least feels organised and a little less lonely, even on days when replies are still frustratingly slow to arrive.",
