    modelMonologue: "The quiet place I want to describe is a small café on the second floor of an old building near my apartment, the kind of spot that's easy to walk past without noticing because the sign out front is tiny and faded.\n\nI discovered it almost by accident when I was looking for somewhere to grade speaking recordings without background noise from my own apartment, where a neighbour's renovation had been going on for weeks. The café itself is deliberately minimal — no background music, dim lighting, a handful of tables spaced far enough apart that conversations don't overlap, and staff who never rush you even after hours of sitting there with a single iced tea.\n\nWhat I like most is that it attracts a specific kind of regular: freelancers with headphones, students revising quietly, occasionally someone reading alone by the window. There's an unspoken understanding that you go there to focus, not to socialise loudly, which suits my personality far better than busier, trendier cafés packed with group photos and background chatter.\n\nI usually go there once or twice a week, especially when I need to plan lessons or write feedback without constant interruption. Something about the specific combination of soft lighting, minimal noise, and being slightly anonymous among strangers helps me concentrate in a way my own living room, however comfortable, rarely manages once notifications start piling up.\n\nIt's not a dramatic or scenic place by any means, but that quiet, slightly unremarkable atmosphere is exactly why I keep returning, and why I'd genuinely recommend it to anyone who needs a calm corner to actually get things done.",
