    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The shop I want to talk about is a small independent bookshop near my apartment, tucked into a quiet alley just off a busier street, which I've been visiting fairly regularly for almost three years now.\n\nIt's nothing like the big chain bookstores you see in shopping malls — no bright fluorescent lighting, no loud background music, just a narrow, slightly cramped space with wooden shelves reaching up to the ceiling and a cat that sleeps on the counter most afternoons. The owner, an older man who clearly knows every book he's stocked, often recommends things based on a two-minute conversation rather than a bestseller list, which has led me to some genuinely unexpected favourites over the years.\n\nI enjoy visiting mainly because it doesn't feel transactional the way online shopping does, even though I'll admit I order most things online for convenience these days. There's something satisfying about physically flipping through pages, catching the specific smell of slightly aging paper, and occasionally overhearing other regulars debating some novel with the owner while I browse nearby.\n\nIt's also become a kind of decompression spot for me. As someone who works from home and rarely leaves the neighbourhood, popping in there for twenty minutes between lessons gives me a small, low-effort change of scenery without requiring any real planning or travel.\n\nI don't buy something every visit — sometimes I just browse and leave empty-handed — but the shop itself, with its slightly chaotic shelving and unhurried atmosphere, has become one of those small fixtures I'd genuinely miss if it ever closed down.",
