    modelMonologue: "The cake I want to describe arrived on my birthday last year, sent by three close friends who'd apparently been messaging each other secretly for weeks without me noticing anything at all.\n\nI was having a fairly ordinary afternoon at home, scrolling through job listings out of habit, when a Grab driver called asking for my exact floor number because he had a delivery marked \"fragile, handle like a baby.\" I honestly assumed it was a food order I'd forgotten about. Instead, it was a small round cake, soft mocha sponge with \"Happy Birthday\" written across the top in slightly wobbly icing, clearly a home-bakery order rather than something from a big commercial chain.\n\nWhat made it special wasn't really the cake itself, which was lovely but modest — no fancy decorations, no extravagant flavours. It was the group-chat screenshot one friend sent afterwards, showing them arguing over which bakery to choose and whether I preferred chocolate or coffee flavour, based apparently on a comment I'd made months earlier that I don't even remember making myself.\n\nI ended up eating half of it that same evening, filming a short thank-you video for our group chat, slightly embarrassed at how touched I felt over something so small. Being between jobs lately has made some days feel oddly quiet and uneventful, so having friends coordinate something that thoughtful, entirely without me around to organise it, genuinely caught me off guard. I still think about that cake whenever I'm having one of those slower, uncertain weeks.",
