    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a story/book with animals in it",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The book I want to talk about is Dế Mèn Phiêu Lưu Ký — Diary of a Cricket — by Tô Hoài, a Vietnamese classic almost every kid here reads in primary school. My copy was actually a hand-me-down from my older cousin, slightly torn cover and all, and I remember reading it under the ceiling fan one summer when there wasn't much else to do.\n\nThe story follows Dế Mèn, a young cricket who's arrogant and a bit of a bully at the start, picking on a weaker neighbour, Dế Choắt, just to show off his strength. Things go badly wrong when one of his careless pranks gets Dế Choắt seriously hurt, and the guilt from that moment pushes Dế Mèn to leave home and wander through the insect world, meeting all sorts of creatures — ants, beetles, even a fearsome mantis — learning humility along the way.\n\nWhat stuck with me wasn't really the adventure part, though that was entertaining enough for a ten-year-old. It was the guilt scene right after Dế Choắt gets hurt; even as a kid, I remember feeling genuinely uncomfortable reading it, which is probably the first time a book made me actually reflect on being unkind to someone smaller or weaker than me.\n\nI still think about that book occasionally, especially when I catch myself getting impatient with a slower student. Tô Hoài managed to build an entire moral lesson using tiny garden insects, which honestly says a lot about good storytelling — you don't need grand animals or exotic settings, just one flawed character who learns something the hard way.",
