    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a thing you did to learn another language",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The language I want to talk about is Chinese, which I started learning about two years ago, mostly out of curiosity rather than any serious career plan. Since I already teach English for a living, I wanted a language that felt genuinely new and a bit humbling, if that makes sense.\n\nThe specific thing I did was download a vocabulary app called HelloChinese and commit to using it for just fifteen minutes every morning before starting work. I know that sounds unremarkable, but the trick was consistency rather than intensity — I never did long marathon sessions, just small, repeated exposure. Alongside that, I started watching Chinese dramas with Vietnamese subtitles in the evenings, partly for entertainment and partly to get used to how the tones actually sound in real speech, not just in app audio clips.\n\nAfter a few months, I noticed I could recognise basic characters and pick out simple phrases in dialogue without relying on the subtitles as much. It was oddly satisfying, especially because Chinese felt so different from English grammar-wise, which forced me to think about language structure in a completely new way. There were plenty of frustrating days too — tones are genuinely difficult, and I'd mix up words that sounded almost identical to my untrained ear.\n\nLooking back, that small daily habit taught me something useful for my own teaching as well: language learning sticks better through steady little doses than through occasional cramming. I'm still far from fluent, but I've kept the habit going, and it's become one of those low-pressure hobbies I actually look forward to rather than dread.",
