    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about chú Bảy, the maintenance guy in my apartment building here in Ho Chi Minh City. He's probably in his fifties, and he's been fixing things around our block for years — leaking pipes, broken lift buttons, that sort of thing. Most residents just call him \"chú sửa đồ\" because nobody remembers his actual title.\n\nThe problem came up a few months ago, right in the middle of an online lesson I was teaching. The water pump on our floor suddenly stopped working, so nobody could get running water, and the building's regular technician was stuck fixing something on the other side of the city. Chú Bảy wasn't even on duty that day, but a couple of neighbours knocked on his door anyway, half-hoping he could do something.\n\nInstead of panicking or calling for an expensive replacement straight away, he checked the pump himself and worked out that the motor hadn't actually broken — the automatic switch had simply tripped from a small power surge earlier that morning. Rather than swapping out the whole unit, which would have cost the building a lot and taken days to arrange, he just reset the switch and ran a short test cycle to make sure it wouldn't trip again. Water was back within twenty minutes.\n\nWhat struck me was how calm he stayed with a dozen annoyed residents watching him work. He didn't reach for the most dramatic fix; he diagnosed the actual cause first. It reminded me that a clever solution isn't always about doing something complicated — sometimes it's just staying observant enough to notice what's really wrong instead of guessing.",
