    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about some advice I gave to my cousin Linh about a year ago, when she was finishing high school and completely torn between two university majors — marketing and information technology.\n\nShe came to me because I'm the only person in the family who works somewhat independently, so she assumed I'd have a more objective view than our parents, who kept pushing her toward whichever major sounded more \"stable.\" When we talked, it became obvious she was actually more drawn to IT — she loved tinkering with apps and had taught herself some basic coding — but she was scared of struggling in a male-dominated field and disappointing relatives who preferred marketing as the \"safer\" choice.\n\nMy advice was fairly simple: choose based on what she could imagine still enjoying after five tedious, ordinary years of it, not based on which major sounded more impressive at family gatherings. I also reminded her that competence usually beats gender stereotypes once you're actually skilled at something, and that switching later is far harder than starting where your genuine interest already is.\n\nShe ended up choosing IT, and although the first year was undeniably tough, she's stuck with it and recently landed a decent internship. Whenever she updates me, I feel a quiet sense of satisfaction — not because I magically knew the right answer, but because I helped her trust her own instinct instead of outside pressure. It reminded me that good advice usually isn't about giving someone a new idea; it's about helping them hear the one they already had.",
