    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The time I want to describe is getting up at half past four in the morning for a video interview with a company abroad, because the time difference left almost no other convenient slot for either of us.\n\nThey'd emailed a few days earlier, explaining that their hiring manager was based overseas and could only join calls during their own working hours, which happened to line up with the middle of the night for early morning here in Ho Chi Minh City. I'm honestly not a natural early riser — left to my own habits, I'd rather stay in and start the day slowly — but the opportunity genuinely interested me, and it was only one interview, not a permanent arrangement.\n\nThe morning itself was rough at first. I set two alarms just in case, made coffee before even opening my laptop, and spent the first few minutes worrying I'd sound half-asleep rather than professional. What surprised me was how quiet and calm the city felt at that hour — no traffic noise below my window, no delivery bikes yet, just streetlights and the occasional stray dog wandering past. It gave the whole morning an oddly focused, almost peaceful atmosphere, like nobody else in the building was awake to interrupt.\n\nThe interview itself went better than I'd expected, and although I didn't end up taking that particular role, waking up early for it taught me something useful: habits I assumed were completely fixed, like being hopeless in the mornings, can bend surprisingly well when there's a clear enough reason behind them.",
