    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about organising a surprise birthday dinner for my mother at home last year, which turned out far better than I expected, considering how last-minute the planning actually was.\n\nHer birthday fell on a weekday, and she'd mentioned casually that she didn't want anything special that year, just a normal quiet evening. That comment was actually what pushed me to plan something anyway, since I felt she deserved more than \"nothing special\" after a stressful few months at work. I only had about four days to arrange everything around my teaching schedule.\n\nI coordinated quietly with my younger sibling to keep her out of the apartment on the actual evening, ordered her favourite dishes through a delivery app instead of attempting to cook everything myself, and asked two of her close friends to drop by, pretending it was just a casual visit. The trickiest part was timing — making sure the food arrived exactly when the guests did, and lying convincingly enough on the phone so she wouldn't suspect anything before walking through the door.\n\nWhen she actually walked in and saw everyone there with a small cake, her reaction was somewhere between confused and genuinely emotional, which made the whole rushed planning worth it. We ended up staying up much later than usual, laughing over old family stories, and she mentioned afterwards that it was one of the birthdays she'd remember clearly. It taught me that a successful event doesn't need to be elaborate or expensive — mostly it just needs the right people gathered with real intention behind it.",
