    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to describe a time I messaged an old high school classmate, Thảo, to suggest a small reunion, and then waited almost two months without hearing anything back.\n\nWe'd been fairly close back in school — same class for two years, used to study together before exams — but like a lot of school friendships, we drifted apart once university and work pulled us in different directions. Last year a mutual friend mentioned that Thảo had actually moved back to Ho Chi Minh City after some years away, so I thought it would be nice to get a small group together, nothing fancy, just coffee and catching up.\n\nI sent her a friendly message through social media, mentioning the idea and asking if she'd be free some weekend. Nothing came back for about a week, which didn't worry me much since people get busy. So I followed up once, lightly, saying there was no rush and we could pick any date that suited her. Still nothing. The message stayed marked as seen, and I started assuming she just wasn't interested anymore, or maybe she rarely checked that account these days.\n\nAlmost two months later, she finally replied, apologising and explaining she'd been dealing with a stressful house move and a new job at the same time, and had genuinely meant to answer sooner. We did eventually meet up, and it was as easy as before, like no time had passed. It taught me that silence online doesn't always mean rejection — sometimes people are simply overwhelmed, and a late reply can still lead to a perfectly normal, warm reunion.",
