    questions: [
      "Cue card: Describe a time when you worked in a group",
    ],
    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to describe a group project I worked on with three colleagues at my previous office job, aimed at reorganising how our department tracked client documents, mostly carried out over about six weeks before I eventually left that role.\n\nWe'd each been managing our own folders and spreadsheets separately for years, which meant duplicated files, inconsistent naming, and constant confusion whenever someone was on leave and a client called about missing paperwork. One of the more senior colleagues suggested we finally build a shared, properly organised system instead, and I volunteered to help coordinate it alongside my usual daily tasks.\n\nThe main challenge was that all four of us had completely different working habits and levels of patience for technology. Two colleagues preferred sticking to familiar spreadsheets, one wanted a fancier database system nobody else understood, and I was juggling client calls throughout most days, so we mostly communicated through a shared document and occasional short meetings rather than constant discussion. There were disagreements too, mainly about how strict the new filing rules should be versus keeping things simple enough for less tech-confident colleagues to actually use.\n\nBy the end, we'd built a genuinely workable system that the department still uses, from what I hear. What struck me most was how much faster progress went once we stopped insisting on our own individual preferences and actually compromised on one shared structure. It also reminded me that group work succeeds less through constant meetings and more through clear roles, patience, and everyone quietly doing their share without needing constant credit.",
