    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The time I want to talk about was when I spent an entire weekend imagining, in surprising detail, exactly what my future bookstore-café would look and feel like, long before I have the money or the actual space to build it.\n\nThis happened a few months ago, on an unusually quiet Saturday with nothing scheduled and no interviews to prepare for. I opened a blank notebook, intending to just jot down a rough business plan, but instead found myself sketching a floor layout: which corner would hold the reading nook, where the coffee counter should sit so the smell doesn't overwhelm the books, and what kind of lighting would make people want to linger rather than rush their drink.\n\nThere was no real template to follow, since I've never actually run a shop before, so I basically had to invent the atmosphere from scratch, imagining how a customer might move through the space, what music would suit quiet afternoons versus busier weekends, even a rough shelf arrangement by genre rather than just alphabetically. It stretched my imagination more than I expected, mostly because small, practical details kept demanding logical answers — where deliveries would be unloaded, how many seats before it felt cramped rather than cosy.\n\nTo my surprise, the exercise felt oddly productive rather than like daydreaming. I ended up with several pages of sketches and notes I still refer back to whenever I save a bit more money toward the idea. It taught me that imagination isn't just for creative hobbies; sometimes it's the only way to test whether a vague dream could actually survive contact with reality.",
