    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The advertisement I want to describe is a Shopee ad that ran heavily around last year's big sale campaign, featuring a well-known Vietnamese singer dancing along to that catchy, slightly repetitive Shopee jingle everyone in Vietnam has heard at least once, whether they wanted to or not.\n\nI saw it constantly — as a skippable pre-roll before YouTube videos, as a sponsored clip on TikTok, even blasting from a phone-shop speaker once while I walked past on an errand. In the ad, the singer, dressed in bright orange to match the brand colour, does a short, deliberately silly dance while flashing discount codes and countdown numbers, encouraging viewers to open the app before the sale timer runs out.\n\nNormally I skip ads within two seconds, but this one stuck partly because of sheer repetition and partly because the singer clearly wasn't taking it too seriously — there's a self-aware, almost mocking energy to it, like even he knows the jingle is objectively annoying yet impossible to forget once it's stuck in your head. My own students would randomly hum it during breaks between speaking practice, which says something about how effective repetition can be.\n\nI remember it mainly because it worked exactly as intended: I ended up opening the app during that same sale, mostly out of habit rather than genuine need, and bought a phone case I probably didn't need. Using a familiar, likeable face to make an annoying jingle bearable turned out to be a surprisingly effective advertising formula, even on someone who actively tries to ignore ads.",
