    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The ambition I want to talk about is being able to actually play the guitar properly, which is honestly a little embarrassing to admit, since I technically \"learned\" it once as a teenager and still can't get through a full song without stumbling.\n\nIt started because learning guitar was extremely trendy among my classmates back then — everyone wanted to look cool strumming something at a school event — so I begged my dad to let me join a beginner class near our house. He wasn't fully convinced it would last, but he let me go anyway, mostly out of curiosity about whether I'd actually stick with it. For a couple of months I practised, quite badly, memorising three or four basic chords.\n\nThe trouble was that I'd joined mainly to keep up with a trend, not because I genuinely loved the instrument. Once the initial excitement faded and my fingers kept hurting from pressing the strings, I quietly stopped attending. The guitar ended up leaning in a corner of my room, gathering dust, and it eventually got given away when my family moved apartments.\n\nThese days, with more free time between jobs than I've had in years, I sometimes tell myself I'll pick it up again through an app, or finally book a few proper lessons instead of drifting through them halfheartedly. Whenever a friend casually plays something at a gathering, I feel a small pang of regret, because it's not that I've lacked the time completely — I simply never treated it as more than a passing trend. It's a strange, unfinished kind of ambition, but it still nags at me whenever a guitar happens to be nearby.",
