    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "If I could introduce one environmental law in Vietnam, it would be a much stricter law on dumping rubbish into canals and drains, with real fines that actually get enforced, not just posted on paper somewhere.\n\nWhere I live, in a fairly ordinary HCMC neighbourhood, the drain near my lane gets clogged almost every rainy season because people toss plastic bags, styrofoam boxes, and food waste straight into the canal instead of waiting for the collection truck. Within twenty minutes of a heavy shower, the water backs up and the whole lane floods — motorbikes wading through ankle-deep water, shop owners scrambling to lift furniture off the floor. It's a small, local example, but multiply that across hundreds of wards and you get the flooding problems the whole city complains about every year.\n\nRight now, there are rules against littering, but enforcement is patchy — people rarely get caught, so the fine feels theoretical rather than real. My proposed law would combine visible penalties, maybe through local patrol officers or simple camera monitoring at known dumping spots, with cheap or free rubbish-collection points, so people have less excuse to dump illegally.\n\nI'll admit most adults around me don't think about this daily; convenience usually wins. But I've noticed some younger residents, students especially, getting more vocal about flooding and plastic waste online, which gives me a bit of hope. If the law made consequences immediate rather than abstract, I think even reluctant neighbours would adjust their habits fairly quickly, simply because getting fined hurts more than a guilty conscience ever does.",
