    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "This one is easy — my cousin's wedding reception about a year ago, held at a banquet hall in District 8. Everything else about the night was lovely: the food was generous, relatives I hadn't seen in ages showed up, and the bride looked stunning. The music, though, was a different story entirely.\n\nThe family had hired a live band that mostly played remixed dance tracks at what felt like double the volume necessary for an indoor hall. I remember sitting at a table near the speakers, and within twenty minutes my ears were practically ringing every time someone tried to make a toast. Conversations turned into shouting matches just to be heard across the table, which sort of defeats the whole point of a family gathering.\n\nWhat annoyed me more was the song choice itself — an odd mix of overly dramatic pop ballads during what should have been light-hearted moments, followed suddenly by loud EDM drops right when the elderly relatives were trying to have a proper toast with the groom's parents. It felt mismatched, as if nobody had actually planned the playlist around the mood of each moment; they'd just grabbed whatever happened to be trending that month.\n\nBy the time the cake-cutting happened, I'd basically given up trying to talk to anyone and just watched from a corner, slightly deafened. I still smile about that wedding because the couple is happy now, but if I'm ever asked to help plan a family event again, the music volume and playlist will be the very first thing I insist on controlling.",
