    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "I want to talk about the decision to quit my office job about eight months ago, without having anything else lined up afterwards. Before that, I'd been working in sales admin for a mid-sized trading company, and on paper it looked perfectly fine — stable salary, colleagues I got along with, a proper title. But the daily reality was quietly wearing me down: long commutes through traffic, repetitive tasks I'd stopped feeling any pride in, and almost zero time to think about what I actually wanted from my life.\n\nThe decision wasn't sudden. I'd been unhappy for months, quietly saving a bit of money just in case, half-hoping the feeling would simply pass on its own. Then one evening, after getting home late and realising I couldn't remember the last time I'd felt genuinely interested in my own work, I sat down and calculated whether I could survive for around six months without a salary. The number wasn't comfortable, but it wasn't impossible either, so I handed in my resignation the following week.\n\nLooking back, I'd call it one of the more important decisions of my adult life, because it changed almost everything — my daily rhythm, my sense of what \"success\" even means to me, even how I spend weekends. The first couple of months were nerve-wracking, with no income and a slightly worried mother checking in constantly. But I finally had space to think properly, explore small business ideas like running a café, and stop pretending a job I disliked was fine just because it looked stable from the outside.",
