    modelMonologue: "The river I want to talk about is the Saigon River, which winds through Ho Chi Minh City and has honestly shaped the whole layout and history of the city I grew up in.\n\nHistorically, it's been central to the city's development — colonial-era buildings, ports, and trading routes all clustered along its banks, and even today a huge amount of cargo still moves through it via barges heading to industrial zones outside the city. Economically, it's not just scenery; it's genuinely functional infrastructure that keeps goods moving without adding to the already chronic road traffic.\n\nMore personally, though, what I associate with the river is the riverside walkway near District 1, where I occasionally go for an evening walk when I need to clear my head after a slow, uncertain day of job-hunting. Watching the water reflect the city lights, with the newer high-rises on the Thủ Thiêm side lit up across the water, gives the place a strange contrast between old trading history and a fairly futuristic-looking skyline.\n\nI think it's important beyond just being pretty, though. There's been growing public conversation about pollution and flooding risks linked to the river, especially as the city keeps expanding and older drainage systems struggle to cope. Some younger residents have started paying more attention to plastic waste along the banks, even if it's not yet a citywide obsession.\n\nFor me, the Saigon River represents something quietly essential rather than dramatic — infrastructure, history, and a rare pocket of calm, all overlapping in a city that otherwise never really slows down.",
