    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The building I want to describe is the old apartment block on Nguyễn Huệ walking street, the one everyone just calls \"the cafe apartment\" building. I visited it properly about eight months ago with a friend who was craving something photogenic for her social media.\n\nFrom the street, it honestly looks unremarkable — a slightly worn, narrow apartment building constructed decades ago, with a shared staircase and laundry occasionally hanging from balconies. But once you walk inside and start climbing, nearly every floor has been converted into a tiny café, boutique, or nail salon, each with a completely different theme, squeezed into what used to be someone's living room.\n\nWhat made it genuinely interesting to me wasn't just the aesthetic, though that's obviously why tourists love it, but the contrast between old and new happening in the exact same physical space. The original tiled floors, ageing wiring, and narrow corridors are still there, clearly decades old, while the businesses inside are aggressively modern — minimalist furniture, neon signs, plants everywhere for the perfect photo. My friend and I climbed to the sixth floor mostly out of curiosity rather than needing coffee, and by the time we reached the top, my legs were tired but I was oddly fascinated by how a residential building had quietly turned itself into a vertical shopping street without demolishing anything.\n\nIt's not \"beautiful\" in a polished, purpose-built way, but that slightly chaotic mix of eras is exactly what makes it memorable, and it's stayed in my mind far longer than plenty of newer, shinier buildings I've walked past in the same district.",
