    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The video I want to describe is a short clip I came across on Facebook a few months ago about an elderly woman who sells bánh mì from a small cart near a university in Hà Nội. Someone had filmed her quietly, without her noticing at first, and posted it with barely any caption.\n\nIn the video, a group of students order bread, and when one boy realises he's short on cash, he starts apologising and trying to put items back. Before he even finishes the sentence, the old woman waves him off, hands him the full sandwich anyway, and says something like, \"Ăn đi, tiền thiếu thì trả sau, đói thì học không vô đâu\" — eat first, pay later if you're short, you can't study properly on an empty stomach. She does this so naturally, like it's routine rather than charity, and the person filming mentioned she'd apparently been doing this quietly for years.\n\nWhat made it interesting to me wasn't dramatic editing or music — there was barely any — but how unremarkable kindness looked on her face, like feeding hungry students was simply part of running her cart, not some special act worth announcing. The comments underneath were full of former students recognising her and sharing similar memories.\n\nI ended up sending that video to two of my own students that same week, partly to make a point about generosity, partly just because it stuck with me. It reminded me that some of the most memorable content online isn't clever or viral by design — it's just an ordinary person being decent, caught on camera by accident.",
