    modelMonologue: "The occasion I want to describe is Tet reunion dinner at my grandmother's house this year, which is honestly one of the few times almost the entire extended family manages to be in the same room at once. We'd all arrived gradually throughout the afternoon — aunts carrying trays of food, cousins arguing playfully over who'd bring the right kind of bánh chưng, my grandmother fussing over whether there was enough space at the table for everyone. By the time we actually sat down to eat, the house was loud in that chaotic, comforting way that only happens once a year, with kids running between adults' legs and someone's phone constantly buzzing with New Year messages. What made people smile so much, I think, wasn't any single joke or event — it was the accumulation of small things happening simultaneously: my uncle telling an exaggerated story about a failed business deal that everyone had heard three times already, my little cousin proudly showing off a lucky money envelope, my grandmother laughing so hard at something that she had to wipe her eyes with her sleeve. Even the awkward small talk with distant relatives you only see once a year felt oddly warm rather than forced. At one point, someone took a group photo, and I remember genuinely struggling to get everyone looking at the camera at the same time because half the table was still laughing at something unrelated. Looking back at that photo now, almost nobody has a \"proper\" smile — they're all mid-laugh, slightly blurred, which somehow makes it my favourite family photo.",
