    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The occasion I want to talk about is the day I sat my IELTS test a couple of years ago. As most test-takers know, phones are strictly banned throughout the entire process, from the moment you check in to the second you walk out of the exam room.\n\nI remember arriving early, feeling reasonably calm, until the invigilator asked everyone to switch off their phones completely and place them in a locker outside the testing room — not just silence them, but hand them over entirely. That small moment actually rattled me more than I expected. I realised how used I'd become to checking my phone constantly, refreshing messages or scrolling mindlessly whenever I felt even slightly nervous, and suddenly that option was gone for nearly three hours straight.\n\nDuring the short breaks between sections, I caught myself instinctively reaching toward my pocket a few times, forgetting there was nothing there. It sounds almost silly now, but that phantom reflex told me something uncomfortable about how dependent I'd become on constant, low-level distraction, even in a moment that required real focus.\n\nIronically, once the initial discomfort passed, I found the total disconnection strangely helpful. Without notifications pulling at my attention, I stayed noticeably more focused during Listening and Reading than I usually am while studying at home with my phone nearby. These days, whenever I catch myself endlessly refreshing job-application emails out of anxiety, I sometimes think back to that exam room and deliberately leave my phone in another room for an hour, just to force myself back into that same uninterrupted focus.",
