    answers: [],
    modelMonologue: "The friend who comes to mind is Trang, someone I've known since university. What she taught herself is actually guitar, which feels a bit ironic to me because I also took a guitar class as a teenager — mostly just because it was trendy at the time — and I still can't play a single song properly to this day.\n\nTrang, on the other hand, never had a single formal lesson. She started around three years ago, during a period when she was stuck at home a lot, and she basically built her whole skill set from YouTube tutorials and random guitar forums. I remember visiting her place once and seeing her phone propped up against a water bottle, replaying the same ten-second clip of a finger-picking pattern over and over, pausing and rewinding until her fingers finally copied it correctly.\n\nWhat impressed me most wasn't just that she learned the chords — plenty of people manage that — but that she taught herself proper technique: posture, finger positioning, even how to tune the instrument by ear instead of relying on an app. She told me the hardest part wasn't the music itself but staying motivated without anyone checking her progress or correcting her mistakes in real time.\n\nNowadays she plays casually at small gatherings, and honestly it's a little humbling to watch, especially remembering my own guitar class that went absolutely nowhere despite having an actual teacher. It makes me think discipline sometimes matters more than formal instruction, though I still haven't picked up a guitar again to test that theory myself.",
